#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056012 :tutorial/content "To solve an equation like\n\n$$\n2x+4 =10\n$$\n\n, we need to apply both the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $4$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2x+4{\\color{red}\\:-\\:4} &=10{\\color{red}\\:-\\:4} \\\\ 2x+0&=6 \\\\ 2x &=6 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{2x}{\\color{red}2} &=\\dfrac{6}{\\color{red}2}\\ \\dfrac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{\\color{red}2}} &= 3 \\\\ x &=3 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=3$ is our solution. Let's plug this back into the original equation, to be sure that it's correct.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 4 &= 10 \\\\\n2 \\cdot 3 + 4 &= 10 \\\\\n6 + 4 &= 10 \\\\\n10 &= 10✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** When solving these types of equations, we always apply the addition principle first, and the multiplication principle second." :tutorial/id #uuid "f8f52cdf-9de7-5b27-addc-4bc35612fa15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8055 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056013 :tutorial/content "Solving equations with both the addition and multiplication principles can be a lot of work. There are often many steps to write down. However, we don't have to write down all of the steps.\n\nFor example, when using the multiplication principle to solve\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y}{2} = 5\n$$\n\nthe set of full steps are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y}{2} &= 5 \\\\\n2 \\cdot \\frac{y}{2} &= 2 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n\\frac{2y}{2} &= 10 \\\\\n\\frac{2y}{2} &= 10 \\\\\ny &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we cancel out the $2$'s to indicate that they disappear, then we can skip writing down several of the steps.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y}{2} &= 5 \\\\\n2 \\cdot \\frac{y}{2} &= 2 \\cdot 5 \\\\\ny &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f3b97f5f-3831-50b9-bb82-d4f1b7b6f8c0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8056 :tutorial/title "Cancellation Notation"}]}
